<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Generative versus reconstructive approaches</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further investigate alternative strategies to verify the necessity of a generative framework for latent-to-waveform generation.
For fair comparison, we employ the same text-to-latent model used in Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Results ‣ SemanticVocoder: Bridging Audio Generation and Audio Understanding via Semantic Latents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, since the introduction of semantic latents as an anchor renders our two-stage pipeline mutually independent and plug-and-play.
We adopt the training framework of the StableAudio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> VAE to reconstruct waveforms from semantic latents.
As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Generative versus reconstructive approaches ‣ SemanticVocoder: Bridging Audio Generation and Audio Understanding via Semantic Latents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, employing a a reconstruction-based approach for latent-to-waveform conversion results in performance degradation, as traditional reconstruction methods suffer from audio distortion when dealing with semantic latents.</p>
</div>
